00:03:11.070 --> 00:03:12.780
So let's recap very quickly.

00:03:12.780 --> 00:03:15.330
And in the recap, I
won't dwell on things

00:03:15.330 --> 00:03:17.610
that you don't need
as much going forward,

00:03:17.610 --> 00:03:20.280
more on the things
that we'll focus on,

00:03:20.280 --> 00:03:22.860
so more on the results,
less on the proofs.

00:03:22.860 --> 00:03:25.110
We learnt about
Kraft's inequality.

00:03:25.110 --> 00:03:27.240
Kraft's inequality was an
if and only if condition

00:03:27.240 --> 00:03:28.110
for prefix codes.

00:03:28.110 --> 00:03:31.770
So you are a prefix code if
and only if your lengths--

00:03:31.770 --> 00:03:33.160
no not really if and only if.

00:03:33.160 --> 00:03:34.910
If you're a prefix
code, then your lengths

00:03:34.910 --> 00:03:36.390
satisfy a certain inequality.

00:03:36.390 --> 00:03:39.090
And if the lengths satisfies
certain inequality,

00:03:39.090 --> 00:03:42.000
then you can create a
prefix code out of it.

00:03:42.000 --> 00:03:43.530
So that was Kraft's inequality.

00:03:43.530 --> 00:03:45.030
We won't need it really.

00:03:45.030 --> 00:03:47.340
You have a question in
homework about proving it

00:03:47.340 --> 00:03:49.770
for uniquely decodable codes.

00:03:49.770 --> 00:03:53.700
So good luck with that one.

00:03:53.700 --> 00:03:54.510
Entropy.

00:03:54.510 --> 00:03:55.650
We defined entropy.

00:03:55.650 --> 00:03:57.660
Entropy is the
fundamental quantity

00:03:57.660 --> 00:03:58.770
for lossless compression.

00:03:58.770 --> 00:04:04.920
It's the lower bound on the
best compression possible.

00:04:04.920 --> 00:04:06.745
And it's defined
as this formula.

00:04:06.745 --> 00:04:08.370
And you had some quiz
questions to help

00:04:08.370 --> 00:04:10.410
you get more familiar with it.

00:04:10.410 --> 00:04:13.360
We talked about joint
entropy for IID variables

00:04:13.360 --> 00:04:16.839
where if you have n IID
variables x1 through xn, then

00:04:16.839 --> 00:04:19.899
their joint entropy, you
can do it in two steps.

00:04:19.899 --> 00:04:22.930
So the first step is that
the joint entropy is the sum

00:04:22.930 --> 00:04:24.130
of the individual entropies.

00:04:24.130 --> 00:04:27.220
This step uses independence
of the random variables.

00:04:27.220 --> 00:04:28.840
And the second
step uses the fact

00:04:28.840 --> 00:04:30.670
that they are
identically distributed

00:04:30.670 --> 00:04:33.220
so that the entropy
of the sum is

00:04:33.220 --> 00:04:35.080
the n times the entropy
of a single one.

00:04:35.080 --> 00:04:37.900
All of them have
the same entropy.

00:04:37.900 --> 00:04:40.015
And then we define
KL divergence.

00:04:40.015 --> 00:04:41.140
I'm very quickly recapping.

00:04:41.140 --> 00:04:43.750
If you have questions,
please let me know.

00:04:43.750 --> 00:04:47.660
So KL divergence was
this other quantity.

00:04:47.660 --> 00:04:50.440
There's two
variables p and q, so

00:04:50.440 --> 00:04:51.880
two probability distributions.

00:04:51.880 --> 00:04:54.490
It's sort of a distance measure.

00:04:54.490 --> 00:05:05.710
So it is greater than or
equal to 0 with equality

00:05:05.710 --> 00:05:10.540
if and only if p is equal to q.

00:05:10.540 --> 00:05:12.850
And you, again, had some
questions in your homework

00:05:12.850 --> 00:05:14.800
about this.

00:05:14.800 --> 00:05:17.170
Then the other big thing
we did last lecture

00:05:17.170 --> 00:05:22.150
was the main result, the real
main theorem of compression

00:05:22.150 --> 00:05:24.527
that for any prefix code--

00:05:24.527 --> 00:05:26.860
in the homework, you will see
for any uniquely decodable

00:05:26.860 --> 00:05:28.310
code--

00:05:28.310 --> 00:05:32.750
the expected code length is
lower bounded by the entropy.

00:05:32.750 --> 00:05:35.120
So you cannot do better
than the entropy.

00:05:35.120 --> 00:05:37.730
And the other part was
that you can actually

00:05:37.730 --> 00:05:39.410
achieve entropy
or get arbitrarily

00:05:39.410 --> 00:05:41.630
close to entropy if
you work with blocks.

00:05:44.530 --> 00:05:46.430
Any questions so
far on last lecture?

00:05:50.300 --> 00:05:51.170
Let's move on.

00:05:53.930 --> 00:05:55.670
Towards the very end
of the last lecture,

00:05:55.670 --> 00:05:58.490
we talked about the
achievability part,

00:05:58.490 --> 00:06:00.680
how do you actually
achieve this.

00:06:00.680 --> 00:06:03.350
So the first thing we did was
we took the Shannon codes.

00:06:03.350 --> 00:06:04.460
We know their lengths.

00:06:04.460 --> 00:06:07.730
We did this calculation with
the ceiling function and so on.

00:06:07.730 --> 00:06:10.310
And what we got was that the
expected length for the Shannon

00:06:10.310 --> 00:06:14.330
code is sandwiched
between entropy of x

00:06:14.330 --> 00:06:16.115
and the entropy of x plus 1.

00:06:19.060 --> 00:06:22.000
And we saw some
examples and you saw one

00:06:22.000 --> 00:06:24.070
in quiz, which we'll see
shortly, where Shannon

00:06:24.070 --> 00:06:25.097
codes didn't do as well.

00:06:25.097 --> 00:06:27.430
You clearly could see that
Shannon codes were very, very

00:06:27.430 --> 00:06:28.070
bad.

00:06:28.070 --> 00:06:31.540
And Shannon codes are
not the optimal codes,

00:06:31.540 --> 00:06:35.320
and we'll look at the optimal
code today, the Huffman codes.

00:06:35.320 --> 00:06:37.180
The other thing we saw
towards the very end

00:06:37.180 --> 00:06:40.270
was that if you
code in blocks of n,

00:06:40.270 --> 00:06:44.350
then the average code
length per symbol

00:06:44.350 --> 00:06:48.510
you can achieve using this
formula we saw just now,

00:06:48.510 --> 00:06:51.390
this one, that the entropy
of xn is equal to n

00:06:51.390 --> 00:06:52.515
times the entropy of x.

00:06:55.550 --> 00:06:59.240
You can get basically
within 1 by n of entropy.

00:06:59.240 --> 00:07:02.780
So working with blocks is really
the way you get arbitrarily

00:07:02.780 --> 00:07:04.460
close to entropy.

00:07:04.460 --> 00:07:07.880
And it's very important
working with blocks.

00:07:07.880 --> 00:07:10.100
You will see as we go
through the lectures

00:07:10.100 --> 00:07:12.950
that there was a
progression in compressors.

00:07:12.950 --> 00:07:15.530
The very early days
compressors like Gzip,

00:07:15.530 --> 00:07:18.170
they used to do Huffman
coding which doesn't

00:07:18.170 --> 00:07:19.550
work with blocks really.

00:07:19.550 --> 00:07:22.430
And they didn't get
very good results.

00:07:22.430 --> 00:07:25.460
And all of the compressors
today, the modern compressors,

00:07:25.460 --> 00:07:27.980
all of them work with blocks.

00:07:27.980 --> 00:07:29.930
But they don't work
in blocks like the way

00:07:29.930 --> 00:07:32.360
you worked in blocks when you
did your homework question.

00:07:32.360 --> 00:07:35.240
There are better ways to
work with blocks, which

00:07:35.240 --> 00:07:36.560
we'll learn about next week.

00:07:36.560 --> 00:07:41.600
So topic for next week
we'll come back to this.

00:07:41.600 --> 00:07:43.610
Let's keep going.

00:07:43.610 --> 00:07:45.560
Let's look at the quiz
which will also help

00:07:45.560 --> 00:07:50.670
motivate some of
today's questions.

00:07:50.670 --> 00:07:52.790
So you have a Bernoulli
p random variable.

00:07:52.790 --> 00:07:54.980
Bernoulli is like one of
the most-- the simplest

00:07:54.980 --> 00:07:55.650
distributions.

00:07:55.650 --> 00:07:56.820
So you have 0 and 1.

00:07:56.820 --> 00:07:58.920
And probability of 1 is p.

00:07:58.920 --> 00:08:02.250
Probability of 0 is 1 minus p.

00:08:02.250 --> 00:08:04.200
So the first question
was, what is the entropy

00:08:04.200 --> 00:08:07.770
of x as a function of p?

00:08:07.770 --> 00:08:08.670
Anybody?

00:08:18.600 --> 00:08:21.820
Anyone wants to--

00:08:21.820 --> 00:08:24.390
I assume all of you did it.

00:08:24.390 --> 00:08:24.890
OK.

00:08:24.890 --> 00:08:26.460
Let me write it down.

00:08:26.460 --> 00:08:28.840
So it is p log--

00:08:28.840 --> 00:08:31.740
it's just as a formula,
nothing very clever.

00:08:35.789 --> 00:08:40.230
So this function,
this is in literature,

00:08:40.230 --> 00:08:42.120
it's sometimes written as h2p.

00:08:42.120 --> 00:08:46.020
Sometimes it's written as hbp,
so binary entropy function.

00:08:46.020 --> 00:08:47.790
That's what it's often called.

00:08:47.790 --> 00:08:52.020
And you can even plot
it as a function of p.

00:08:52.020 --> 00:08:54.740
Let's call it as hbp.

00:08:54.740 --> 00:08:56.000
So what is the value at 0?

00:08:56.000 --> 00:08:58.190
What is the entropy
of Bernoulli 0?

00:09:06.160 --> 00:09:06.750
Zero.

00:09:06.750 --> 00:09:07.250
Yeah.

00:09:07.250 --> 00:09:10.030
I see some people saying
zero, which is correct.

00:09:10.030 --> 00:09:13.450
We learnt last lecture
that the entropy,

00:09:13.450 --> 00:09:16.930
if you have a random variable
which is deterministic,

00:09:16.930 --> 00:09:18.252
its entropy is 0.

00:09:18.252 --> 00:09:19.210
There is no randomness.

00:09:19.210 --> 00:09:22.330
Bernoulli 0 means that
it's always equal to 0.

00:09:22.330 --> 00:09:23.410
It's never 1.

00:09:23.410 --> 00:09:26.440
So the entropy is 0 at 0.

00:09:26.440 --> 00:09:29.590
How about the entropy at 1?

00:09:29.590 --> 00:09:31.810
When p equal to
1, so p equal to 1

00:09:31.810 --> 00:09:35.660
means that it's always equal
to 1, never equal to 0.

00:09:35.660 --> 00:09:36.750
Also zero.

00:09:36.750 --> 00:09:37.250
Yeah.

00:09:37.250 --> 00:09:38.900
A few people said also zero.

00:09:38.900 --> 00:09:40.490
You can put it in the formula.

00:09:40.490 --> 00:09:43.010
Remember that p log 1
by p is defined as 0

00:09:43.010 --> 00:09:44.870
when p is equal to 0.

00:09:44.870 --> 00:09:46.460
So it's also 0 at 1.

00:09:46.460 --> 00:09:47.510
How about at 1/2?

00:09:51.210 --> 00:09:51.710
Yeah.

00:09:51.710 --> 00:09:53.690
For 1/2, I hear 1.

00:09:53.690 --> 00:09:58.670
So 1/2 means that it's
equally likely to be 0 or 1.

00:09:58.670 --> 00:10:02.330
So then the information
content is one bit.

00:10:02.330 --> 00:10:04.760
It's either 0 or 1
with equal probability.

00:10:04.760 --> 00:10:07.200
So it's 1 at 1/2.

00:10:07.200 --> 00:10:12.750
And you can prove in your
information theory course

00:10:12.750 --> 00:10:15.570
that it's a concave function.

00:10:15.570 --> 00:10:18.390
So it looks something like this.

00:10:18.390 --> 00:10:21.630
It's symmetric around 1/2 as
you can see in the expression

00:10:21.630 --> 00:10:23.640
as well.

00:10:23.640 --> 00:10:27.450
And yeah not much
to say other than--

00:10:27.450 --> 00:10:29.280
you will see this
function a few times.

00:10:29.280 --> 00:10:32.520
If you do information theory,
you will see it a bit more.

00:10:32.520 --> 00:10:35.530
It's very flattish
around 1/2 you will see.

00:10:35.530 --> 00:10:37.770
So whether you have a
Bernoulli 1/2 variable

00:10:37.770 --> 00:10:40.350
or you have a Bernoulli
0.4 or Bernoulli 0.6,

00:10:40.350 --> 00:10:42.900
you will see it hardly
makes a difference in how

00:10:42.900 --> 00:10:45.090
compressible your file becomes.

00:10:45.090 --> 00:10:47.400
So there are some practical
observations around

00:10:47.400 --> 00:10:50.640
entropies concavity that
you might encounter.

00:10:53.920 --> 00:10:55.240
OK.

00:10:55.240 --> 00:10:57.520
Please stop me if any questions.

00:10:57.520 --> 00:11:04.045
Then we asked you to do the KL
divergence between Bernoulli

00:11:04.045 --> 00:11:06.670
p and Bernoulli q.

00:11:06.670 --> 00:11:10.590
I will just write
down the expression.

00:11:10.590 --> 00:11:16.220
Please talk to me later if
you had any trouble, again

00:11:16.220 --> 00:11:18.730
just following the formula.

00:11:23.470 --> 00:11:24.790
OK.

00:11:24.790 --> 00:11:26.810
Is this correct?

00:11:26.810 --> 00:11:27.310
OK.

00:11:27.310 --> 00:11:27.940
I see some nods.

00:11:27.940 --> 00:11:28.440
Good.

00:11:28.440 --> 00:11:29.680
OK.

00:11:29.680 --> 00:11:33.710
And then we asked you to
compute this Bernoulli 0.3 0.7.

00:11:33.710 --> 00:11:35.230
I assume you computed it.

00:11:35.230 --> 00:11:38.380
Just plug it in the formula.

00:11:38.380 --> 00:11:40.540
So you will see the quiz
questions are mostly

00:11:40.540 --> 00:11:42.400
meant to make sure
you're following

00:11:42.400 --> 00:11:43.510
what's happening in class.

00:11:43.510 --> 00:11:46.390
They're not-- most
of the time, it's

00:11:46.390 --> 00:11:49.735
not supposed to require
very advanced thinking.

00:11:53.300 --> 00:11:53.840
OK.

00:11:53.840 --> 00:11:57.650
This one, so what we asked
for is, what is the maximum KL

00:11:57.650 --> 00:11:59.750
divergence between
Bernoulli p and Bernoulli

00:11:59.750 --> 00:12:04.460
q if you are allowed to vary
p and q in their entire range

00:12:04.460 --> 00:12:06.110
of 0 to 1?

00:12:06.110 --> 00:12:07.880
What was the answer
that people got?

00:12:17.740 --> 00:12:21.180
Now I hear infinity.

00:12:21.180 --> 00:12:21.750
Yes.

00:12:21.750 --> 00:12:22.250
Yes.

00:12:22.250 --> 00:12:24.540
So the KL divergence
can be unbounded.

00:12:24.540 --> 00:12:25.650
It can be infinity.

00:12:25.650 --> 00:12:30.600
And in this expression
on the last page,

00:12:30.600 --> 00:12:43.990
if you set q to 0 and p to 1,
then you get 1 log infinity.

00:12:47.840 --> 00:12:48.340
Yeah.

00:12:48.340 --> 00:12:49.780
So this is infinity.

00:12:53.320 --> 00:12:54.070
Not much to say.

00:12:54.070 --> 00:12:55.510
It's like it goes from 0.

00:12:55.510 --> 00:12:56.260
Minimum is 0.

00:12:56.260 --> 00:12:57.850
When p equal to q, it's 0.

00:12:57.850 --> 00:13:01.570
And when one of them is 0,
then it becomes infinity.

00:13:04.410 --> 00:13:04.980
Yeah.

00:13:04.980 --> 00:13:07.590
You will see that between
probability distributions,

00:13:07.590 --> 00:13:11.490
there are two very common
ways to measure the distance.

00:13:11.490 --> 00:13:12.960
One of them is KL divergence.

00:13:12.960 --> 00:13:15.810
The other one is something
called total variation distance

00:13:15.810 --> 00:13:18.360
or L1 distance.

00:13:18.360 --> 00:13:21.060
So depending on what
application you're working with,

00:13:21.060 --> 00:13:23.130
you either prefer
one or the other.

00:13:23.130 --> 00:13:25.177
The total variation
thing is symmetric.

00:13:25.177 --> 00:13:26.010
It's never infinite.

00:13:26.010 --> 00:13:26.580
It's bounded.

00:13:26.580 --> 00:13:30.600
It's much nicer in
terms of its properties,

00:13:30.600 --> 00:13:33.660
but this one often works better
in many of the machine learning

00:13:33.660 --> 00:13:37.040
applications.

00:13:37.040 --> 00:13:39.260
And this one is no.

00:13:39.260 --> 00:13:40.550
It is not symmetric.

00:13:40.550 --> 00:13:42.020
You can try out any example.

00:13:42.020 --> 00:13:46.460
Even if you take p equal
to 0.3, q equal to 0.7,

00:13:46.460 --> 00:13:50.200
you will see that it
will be symmetric.

00:13:50.200 --> 00:13:53.800
But if you take this thing,
then it won't be symmetric.

00:13:56.630 --> 00:13:57.130
OK.

00:13:57.130 --> 00:13:59.110
Any questions on
question 1 in the quiz?

00:14:02.670 --> 00:14:05.078
Let's move on.

00:14:05.078 --> 00:14:07.620
This one, this one will be more
interesting for this lecture.

00:14:11.140 --> 00:14:16.030
So you have a Bernoulli 0.001.

00:14:16.030 --> 00:14:19.150
So it's very high probability,
it's a 0 and very low

00:14:19.150 --> 00:14:20.440
probability that it's a 1.

00:14:20.440 --> 00:14:25.150
We asked you to make a
Shannon code for this thing.

00:14:25.150 --> 00:14:29.860
So you can do this calculation.

00:14:29.860 --> 00:14:31.690
You have probability of x.

00:14:31.690 --> 00:14:33.400
And you can
calculate the length,

00:14:33.400 --> 00:14:39.430
which is log 1 over probability
of x, the ceiling function.

00:14:39.430 --> 00:14:42.670
And you get 1 for
0 and 10 for 1.

00:14:42.670 --> 00:14:44.350
So you can now
assign the codewords.

00:14:44.350 --> 00:14:55.180
Maybe you call it 1 0 and
you call it 100000000.

00:14:55.180 --> 00:14:57.460
And then you can calculate
the expected code length,

00:14:57.460 --> 00:15:02.860
which is just 0.999
multiplied by 1 plus 0.001

00:15:02.860 --> 00:15:03.865
multiplied by 10.

00:15:06.910 --> 00:15:09.601
I hope all of you got this.

00:15:09.601 --> 00:15:12.685
This is just-- we have been
doing this for a few lectures.

00:15:16.580 --> 00:15:18.560
Then we asked you to
compute the entropy.

00:15:18.560 --> 00:15:20.990
Again, the entropy, we
already did in question 1.

00:15:20.990 --> 00:15:22.490
You know how to
compute the entropy.

00:15:22.490 --> 00:15:24.650
And then we asked you
to compute expectation

00:15:24.650 --> 00:15:26.510
of the length
divided by entropy.

00:15:26.510 --> 00:15:30.890
And I got around 88.

00:15:30.890 --> 00:15:34.640
I saw some students, I
think, we asked, Pulkit,

00:15:34.640 --> 00:15:37.130
in the question, this
one, the second one,

00:15:37.130 --> 00:15:41.780
to give your answer
to two decimal places.

00:15:41.780 --> 00:15:44.930
And then if you take both the
expected length and the entropy

00:15:44.930 --> 00:15:47.510
to two decimal and then you
divide them, you don't get it.

00:15:47.510 --> 00:15:49.400
You get 100 or
something like that.

00:15:49.400 --> 00:15:51.710
So we can, I guess,
make a regret request.

00:15:51.710 --> 00:15:54.905
And we will look at it
on an individual basis.

00:15:59.420 --> 00:16:01.760
So the takeaway really is
that for this very skewed

00:16:01.760 --> 00:16:04.910
distribution, Shannon codes are
not doing particularly well.

00:16:04.910 --> 00:16:07.470
You should be spending
0.01 bits per symbol.

00:16:07.470 --> 00:16:09.470
You should be spending
very few bits per symbol,

00:16:09.470 --> 00:16:13.100
but you are spending more
than one bit per symbol.

00:16:13.100 --> 00:16:15.995
It's like a huge
overhead, not great.

00:16:18.760 --> 00:16:19.280
OK.

00:16:19.280 --> 00:16:20.300
So any suggestions?

00:16:20.300 --> 00:16:22.655
What is the first thing we
can do to improve this code?

00:16:26.090 --> 00:16:31.320
We can cut down the code
[INAUDIBLE] 1 just after 1.

00:16:31.320 --> 00:16:31.820
Yeah.

00:16:31.820 --> 00:16:35.630
So the first suggestion is that
this clearly seems suboptimal.

00:16:35.630 --> 00:16:37.640
Why do we have this huge thing?

00:16:37.640 --> 00:16:39.600
So Shannon codes are good.

00:16:39.600 --> 00:16:42.200
They helped us prove something,
but they are not optimal

00:16:42.200 --> 00:16:44.750
as we have been seeing
for a while now.

00:16:44.750 --> 00:16:47.180
So you can cut this down.

00:16:47.180 --> 00:16:50.510
What will be the expected
length after you cut this down?

00:16:53.640 --> 00:16:54.140
One.

00:16:54.140 --> 00:16:58.020
The expected will be one because
both of the things are one.

00:16:58.020 --> 00:17:00.000
So it doesn't really
solve our problem.

00:17:00.000 --> 00:17:03.884
Our entropy is 0.011, and we are
still using one bit per symbol.

00:17:07.540 --> 00:17:10.158
So this is the thing I just--
this is the optimal code.

00:17:10.158 --> 00:17:11.200
This is the optimal code.

00:17:11.200 --> 00:17:12.400
You can't do better than this.

00:17:12.400 --> 00:17:13.775
If you have two
symbols, you have

00:17:13.775 --> 00:17:15.520
to spend at least
one bit per symbol.

00:17:15.520 --> 00:17:19.660
So the optimal code here
is not particularly good.

00:17:19.660 --> 00:17:23.410
It's one bit per symbol,
much, much bigger

00:17:23.410 --> 00:17:30.795
than the 0.011 of the entropy.

00:17:30.795 --> 00:17:33.170
So the other thing we have
been seeing for a few lectures

00:17:33.170 --> 00:17:35.300
is to do block coding.

00:17:35.300 --> 00:17:37.250
So let's write it down.

00:17:37.250 --> 00:17:40.680
What does block
coding really mean?

00:17:40.680 --> 00:17:45.180
So you create this joint
symbol, take x1 and x2,

00:17:45.180 --> 00:17:47.260
two symbols at a time.

00:17:47.260 --> 00:17:50.970
So you have four
possibilities 00 01 10 11.

00:17:50.970 --> 00:17:53.460
Then you compute the
probability of these pairs.

00:17:56.093 --> 00:17:57.510
How do you compute
the probability

00:17:57.510 --> 00:18:00.780
of a pair from the probability
of the individual--

00:18:00.780 --> 00:18:04.320
from the original distribution?

00:18:04.320 --> 00:18:10.990
How do you get like p is
01 in terms of p0 and p1?

00:18:10.990 --> 00:18:11.980
I'm hearing product.

00:18:11.980 --> 00:18:17.190
Can anybody explain why product
is the correct way to do it?

00:18:17.190 --> 00:18:20.730
Can I always assume that
product of two things

00:18:20.730 --> 00:18:23.400
is the joint thing?

00:18:23.400 --> 00:18:25.920
What is the assumption, the
underlying assumption here?

00:18:25.920 --> 00:18:28.200
They're independent.

00:18:28.200 --> 00:18:28.700
Yeah.

00:18:28.700 --> 00:18:31.400
So then set is
they're independent.

00:18:31.400 --> 00:18:40.850
So this thing, so px1,x2 is
equal to px1 times px2 due

00:18:40.850 --> 00:18:41.720
to independence.

00:18:41.720 --> 00:18:44.720
So we'll assume independence
for the next couple of weeks,

00:18:44.720 --> 00:18:48.200
and then we'll move on.

00:18:48.200 --> 00:18:48.980
Independence.

00:18:52.750 --> 00:18:56.230
So I did that calculation
at home, got these numbers.

00:18:56.230 --> 00:18:58.480
I made the code.

00:18:58.480 --> 00:18:59.657
This is not a Shannon code.

00:18:59.657 --> 00:19:01.990
This is actually the optimal
code for this distribution,

00:19:01.990 --> 00:19:03.940
and we'll learn how
to make this later on.

00:19:03.940 --> 00:19:05.440
But you can see it makes sense.

00:19:05.440 --> 00:19:08.710
You give short codewords to
the high probability stuff,

00:19:08.710 --> 00:19:12.640
and you give longer codewords
to the low probability stuff,

00:19:12.640 --> 00:19:14.260
hopefully intuitive.

00:19:14.260 --> 00:19:18.500
And then you can compute
the expected length.

00:19:18.500 --> 00:19:20.150
And when you compute
it always remember

00:19:20.150 --> 00:19:23.510
to divide it at the end
by 2 because now you're

00:19:23.510 --> 00:19:24.920
doing two symbols at a time.

00:19:24.920 --> 00:19:26.930
You're doing a block
of two symbols.

00:19:26.930 --> 00:19:29.225
So I did this calculation again.

00:19:31.910 --> 00:19:35.180
And now you see it's actually
closer to the entropy.

00:19:35.180 --> 00:19:40.010
It's 0.5 instead of
one we had before.

00:19:40.010 --> 00:19:41.460
Still very far away.

00:19:41.460 --> 00:19:45.590
0.5 is still roughly 50
times worse than the entropy.

00:19:45.590 --> 00:19:47.810
Long ways to go.

00:19:47.810 --> 00:19:50.090
And really you can keep
increasing the block size,

00:19:50.090 --> 00:19:51.920
but it gets unwieldy very soon.

00:19:51.920 --> 00:19:55.010
The real solution is to
use streaming codes, which

00:19:55.010 --> 00:19:57.320
we'll learn about next week.

00:19:57.320 --> 00:20:00.530
But for now, let's try
to understand how did

00:20:00.530 --> 00:20:02.060
we even get this optimal code.

00:20:02.060 --> 00:20:03.950
It's not trivial.

00:20:03.950 --> 00:20:05.630
I give you any
random distribution.

00:20:05.630 --> 00:20:07.760
We saw Shannon codes
often do very badly.

00:20:07.760 --> 00:20:09.350
How do I get the optimal code?

00:20:09.350 --> 00:20:13.670
And that's the topic
for this lecture.

00:20:13.670 --> 00:20:16.700
So we'll start with some
conditions for optimal prefix

00:20:16.700 --> 00:20:21.530
codes, then we'll look
at the actual code.

00:20:21.530 --> 00:20:25.070
Then we'll talk about
Huffman coding in practice.

00:20:25.070 --> 00:20:29.020
And you will hear some
interesting things

00:20:29.020 --> 00:20:32.780
which you might have heard
in your algorithms class.

00:20:32.780 --> 00:20:35.590
So hopefully, this is fun.

00:20:35.590 --> 00:20:39.780
Any questions so far
before we get started?

00:20:39.780 --> 00:20:40.440
OK.

00:20:40.440 --> 00:20:41.220
Let's get started.

00:20:50.670 --> 00:20:54.510
So optimal prefix codes.

00:20:57.400 --> 00:21:00.070
So when we talk about optimal
things, first thing to define

00:21:00.070 --> 00:21:01.980
is, what do we mean?

00:21:01.980 --> 00:21:03.640
What does it mean to be optimal?

00:21:03.640 --> 00:21:06.400
So in our case,
optimal means we want

00:21:06.400 --> 00:21:13.610
to find the minimum lengths
such that you want to minimize--

00:21:13.610 --> 00:21:15.740
you want to find the
length such that this sum,

00:21:15.740 --> 00:21:18.650
the expected code
length is minimized,

00:21:18.650 --> 00:21:28.400
subject to the prefix free
condition or equivalently

00:21:28.400 --> 00:21:32.360
satisfying Kraft's inequality,
which we saw are equivalent.

00:21:35.560 --> 00:21:38.890
So this is the definition
of optimal prefix codes.

00:21:38.890 --> 00:21:44.920
And those of you who have done
convex optimization or just

00:21:44.920 --> 00:21:48.700
generally optimization, you
might want to go that way.

00:21:48.700 --> 00:21:51.520
And actually if you do
that, you will actually

00:21:51.520 --> 00:21:53.192
start getting some
of the thumb rules

00:21:53.192 --> 00:21:54.400
that we have been looking at.

00:21:54.400 --> 00:21:56.980
The length should
be log 1 over p.

00:21:56.980 --> 00:21:59.980
In the notes, you will see
that if you do the Lagrange

00:21:59.980 --> 00:22:02.590
multiplier stuff for
this one and you proceed,

00:22:02.590 --> 00:22:07.120
you will actually get the thumb
rule that we know and love.

00:22:07.120 --> 00:22:09.790
But today, we will look at
a different way of doing it.

00:22:09.790 --> 00:22:23.960
So we will define
some conditions

00:22:23.960 --> 00:22:39.170
for optimality after
which it is story time.

00:22:39.170 --> 00:22:41.240
So there are two
conditions for optimality.

00:22:41.240 --> 00:22:51.340
One, this one you
have already seen,

00:22:51.340 --> 00:22:52.843
if the probability
of a symbol is

00:22:52.843 --> 00:22:54.760
bigger than the probability
of another symbol.

00:22:54.760 --> 00:22:57.480
You should give it a shorter
or equal length codeword.

00:23:02.320 --> 00:23:06.508
Otherwise, what
you can do is swap.

00:23:06.508 --> 00:23:08.050
Basically, you can
swap the codewords

00:23:08.050 --> 00:23:13.260
and get a better
expected length.

00:23:13.260 --> 00:23:15.210
So this one we
have already seen.

00:23:15.210 --> 00:23:16.620
Let's look at the other one.

00:23:19.740 --> 00:23:36.680
The two longest codewords
has the same length.

00:23:36.680 --> 00:23:40.940
This one we have
not seen before.

00:23:40.940 --> 00:23:46.130
Can anybody-- let
me draw a chord,

00:23:46.130 --> 00:23:48.905
and then we'll see
what I mean here.

00:23:54.710 --> 00:23:59.680
So basically it's
this guy and this guy.

00:23:59.680 --> 00:24:02.560
These are the two longest
codewords for this small code

00:24:02.560 --> 00:24:04.870
that I've drawn.

00:24:04.870 --> 00:24:06.858
They do not have
the same length.

00:24:06.858 --> 00:24:07.900
One of them has length 2.

00:24:07.900 --> 00:24:09.850
One of them has length 3.

00:24:09.850 --> 00:24:13.210
And that means this
cannot be an optimal code.

00:24:13.210 --> 00:24:13.780
Why is that?

00:24:13.780 --> 00:24:14.695
Can anybody explain?

00:24:20.860 --> 00:24:23.590
You can move the
longest one leg up.

00:24:26.540 --> 00:24:27.040
OK.

00:24:27.040 --> 00:24:29.540
So the one suggestion is
that it is not optimal

00:24:29.540 --> 00:24:31.915
because you can take the
longest one and make it shorter.

00:24:35.630 --> 00:24:37.610
And I guess in the
diagram, it's clear.

00:24:37.610 --> 00:24:39.980
But in general, can you
argue why that will still

00:24:39.980 --> 00:24:41.510
leave the code as prefix free?

00:24:47.060 --> 00:24:50.630
Because the longest two, the
difference between one bit.

00:24:50.630 --> 00:24:54.020
The last bit would
allow you to create

00:24:54.020 --> 00:24:58.230
two codewords of the same
length, so the length is same.

00:24:58.230 --> 00:24:58.730
Yeah.

00:24:58.730 --> 00:25:01.190
So basically the answer
is that if you have one

00:25:01.190 --> 00:25:03.830
codeword-- the longest two
codewords are not equal.

00:25:03.830 --> 00:25:06.050
So the longest codeword,
you can basically

00:25:06.050 --> 00:25:10.310
shorten it to the length of
the second longest codeword.

00:25:10.310 --> 00:25:13.820
And do the math at home
in peace basically.

00:25:13.820 --> 00:25:17.540
You will see that it cannot
violate the prefix code

00:25:17.540 --> 00:25:18.270
condition.

00:25:18.270 --> 00:25:18.770
Why?

00:25:18.770 --> 00:25:28.110
Because if the longest code was
a suffix of some other code,

00:25:28.110 --> 00:25:29.070
then--

00:25:29.070 --> 00:25:33.510
Basically, let's say after
shortening the longest code,

00:25:33.510 --> 00:25:35.310
it becomes a suffix
of some other code.

00:25:35.310 --> 00:25:37.680
You can argue that
it has to be already

00:25:37.680 --> 00:25:39.360
a suffix in the original code.

00:25:39.360 --> 00:25:43.050
So it's like-- let
me write it down,

00:25:43.050 --> 00:25:46.800
and then I guess you
can think about it more.

00:25:46.800 --> 00:25:52.140
Why still-- so basically, the
solution is to cut this part.

00:25:52.140 --> 00:25:55.970
Why still prefix free?

00:25:55.970 --> 00:25:56.820
Can you see it?

00:25:56.820 --> 00:25:57.320
Yeah.

00:26:00.070 --> 00:26:15.670
If shortening violates
prefix property,

00:26:15.670 --> 00:26:25.200
you can argue that original
code also violates.

00:26:27.900 --> 00:26:30.690
If you shorten it and then
it starts violating suddenly

00:26:30.690 --> 00:26:33.210
the prefix quality, you can
argue that the original code

00:26:33.210 --> 00:26:35.550
must have been already
violating it, otherwise,

00:26:35.550 --> 00:26:38.730
it's not possible that the
shortening violates it.

00:26:38.730 --> 00:26:41.340
Think about it it's
not very crucial,

00:26:41.340 --> 00:26:43.620
but really these
observations, you

00:26:43.620 --> 00:26:49.500
will see that Shannon code, the
one we saw a few slides ago,

00:26:49.500 --> 00:26:52.650
extra dividend, but
the Shannon code often

00:26:52.650 --> 00:26:55.230
doesn't have this condition.

00:26:55.230 --> 00:26:58.740
Therefore, it is
not always optimal.

00:26:58.740 --> 00:27:01.200
Any questions so far?

00:27:01.200 --> 00:27:02.250
I guess you understand.

00:27:02.250 --> 00:27:04.530
Just understand the
conditions, and the proofs

00:27:04.530 --> 00:27:07.230
are in the book plus you
can think about this.

00:27:07.230 --> 00:27:09.105
This is relatively
easy to reason about.

00:27:13.630 --> 00:27:16.210
Story time.

00:27:16.210 --> 00:27:17.830
Huffman codes.

00:27:17.830 --> 00:27:22.905
So David Huffman was,
I think, a PhD student.

00:27:25.958 --> 00:27:28.000
And he was taking a class
on information theory--

00:27:28.000 --> 00:27:29.042
this was very early days.

00:27:29.042 --> 00:27:34.420
This was a few years after
Shannon had done his 1948 paper

00:27:34.420 --> 00:27:38.410
and Robert Fano, another very
famous information theorist

00:27:38.410 --> 00:27:41.060
was the person teaching that
information theory class.

00:27:41.060 --> 00:27:44.320
So he gave students an option,
either you do a final exam

00:27:44.320 --> 00:27:47.170
or you do a project.

00:27:47.170 --> 00:27:49.750
And true story, it's not a tale.

00:27:49.750 --> 00:27:50.800
It's a true story.

00:27:50.800 --> 00:27:55.600
So Huffman thought
why do the final--

00:27:55.600 --> 00:27:56.560
why do the exam?

00:27:56.560 --> 00:27:57.640
I will do the project.

00:27:57.640 --> 00:28:00.580
It will be more fun
and easier, he thought.

00:28:00.580 --> 00:28:03.880
And the question was
basically [INAUDIBLE]

00:28:03.880 --> 00:28:06.580
find the optimal prefix code.

00:28:06.580 --> 00:28:08.928
And Huffman worked very hard.

00:28:08.928 --> 00:28:10.720
At some point he thought
it's not possible,

00:28:10.720 --> 00:28:12.130
but then he cracked it.

00:28:12.130 --> 00:28:15.370
He solved the problem.

00:28:15.370 --> 00:28:18.605
And then the Professor
Fano told the students

00:28:18.605 --> 00:28:20.230
that this was actually
an open problem.

00:28:20.230 --> 00:28:25.120
He had just given an open
problem as a project problem.

00:28:25.120 --> 00:28:27.940
So yeah, Huffman
at some point later

00:28:27.940 --> 00:28:30.310
said that if he had known
that it was an open problem,

00:28:30.310 --> 00:28:31.977
he probably wouldn't
have even tried it.

00:28:31.977 --> 00:28:33.340
He would have just given up.

00:28:33.340 --> 00:28:37.990
So sometimes not knowing
that it's a tough problem--

00:28:37.990 --> 00:28:41.740
sometimes we underestimate
our abilities.

00:28:41.740 --> 00:28:44.050
And the construction
is very easy.

00:28:44.050 --> 00:28:47.920
It's a greedy construction,
which we'll see in a minute.

00:28:47.920 --> 00:28:50.710
And Fano also had a
greedy construction,

00:28:50.710 --> 00:28:52.893
but that was not
optimal in many cases.

00:28:52.893 --> 00:28:54.310
But Huffman's
greedy construction,

00:28:54.310 --> 00:28:56.770
which we will learn about
is actually optimal.

00:28:56.770 --> 00:29:01.420
So let's look at the final
project of a student.

00:29:09.770 --> 00:29:11.660
So what I'm going to
do is I will write down

00:29:11.660 --> 00:29:16.100
the construction for
your future reference,

00:29:16.100 --> 00:29:18.163
then we'll do one
example, which I will do.

00:29:18.163 --> 00:29:19.580
And then we'll do
another example,

00:29:19.580 --> 00:29:21.380
which you will help me with.

00:29:21.380 --> 00:29:24.420
And then we'll look at
some real life examples.

00:29:24.420 --> 00:29:29.810
So first step is
to create a list

00:29:29.810 --> 00:29:35.550
of nodes which is equal to--

00:29:35.550 --> 00:29:40.900
you have each symbol
and its probability.

00:29:40.900 --> 00:29:43.880
So you make a list like this.

00:29:43.880 --> 00:29:54.715
Then while more
than one node left--

00:29:59.500 --> 00:30:02.870
we'll post the slides as you
might have observed, no need

00:30:02.870 --> 00:30:05.780
to write if you don't want to--

00:30:05.780 --> 00:30:19.660
you pick two nodes
with least probability,

00:30:19.660 --> 00:30:22.740
merge the two nodes.

00:30:22.740 --> 00:30:24.097
And what does merge mean?

00:30:24.097 --> 00:30:25.305
And we'll look at an example.

00:30:29.530 --> 00:30:31.570
Create new node.

00:30:34.310 --> 00:30:36.800
Actually, how many of you
have seen a Huffman code

00:30:36.800 --> 00:30:39.500
in the past?

00:30:39.500 --> 00:30:43.172
May I ask, where
have you seen it?

00:30:43.172 --> 00:30:46.820
In 106b, our final
project, one of the final

00:30:46.820 --> 00:30:48.650
homeworks we're going
to do [INAUDIBLE]..

00:30:48.650 --> 00:30:49.240
I see.

00:30:49.240 --> 00:30:51.650
I see.

00:30:51.650 --> 00:30:54.540
How about you?

00:30:54.540 --> 00:30:56.350
Where I saw--

00:30:56.350 --> 00:30:58.360
Huffman code.

00:30:58.360 --> 00:31:02.600
In my undergrad
course at Berkeley.

00:31:02.600 --> 00:31:03.100
Right.

00:31:03.100 --> 00:31:03.580
Yeah.

00:31:03.580 --> 00:31:04.955
So we'll see that
Huffman code is

00:31:04.955 --> 00:31:08.380
one of the classic examples
of greedy algorithms, which

00:31:08.380 --> 00:31:11.750
you study in many
computer science classes.

00:31:11.750 --> 00:31:14.110
So yeah, for those
who have not seen,

00:31:14.110 --> 00:31:16.750
this is a good introduction.

00:31:16.750 --> 00:31:25.640
So we create a new node with
the two nodes as children.

00:31:30.610 --> 00:31:38.380
Probability equal to sum
of probability of children.

00:31:44.330 --> 00:31:46.490
And you keep on doing this.

00:31:46.490 --> 00:31:47.435
It's like a loop.

00:31:53.390 --> 00:32:05.950
And the last remaining
node is the root.

00:32:11.500 --> 00:32:21.360
And one asterisk, you
break ties arbitrarily.

00:32:25.930 --> 00:32:28.030
So basically, you
start with a list.

00:32:28.030 --> 00:32:33.280
You keep merging the two lowest
probability nodes and you end

00:32:33.280 --> 00:32:36.730
up with a tree where all
of your initial symbols

00:32:36.730 --> 00:32:37.975
are at the leaf of the tree.

00:32:41.102 --> 00:32:42.060
Let's do some examples.

00:32:42.060 --> 00:32:44.490
I think that's the most
useful thing to do.

00:32:50.680 --> 00:32:56.510
Huffman 1.

00:33:19.640 --> 00:33:23.310
So the first step here is you
take the two lowest probability

00:33:23.310 --> 00:33:23.810
nodes.

00:33:23.810 --> 00:33:25.340
So right now, there
are four nodes.

00:33:25.340 --> 00:33:28.340
The initial nodes
are just the symbols.

00:33:28.340 --> 00:33:32.000
You take the two lowest
ones, you merge them.

00:33:32.000 --> 00:33:33.530
What does merging mean?

00:33:33.530 --> 00:33:39.720
You basically take these
two nodes, you merge them,

00:33:39.720 --> 00:33:41.220
and that creates a new node.

00:33:41.220 --> 00:33:43.620
Call it N1, let's say.

00:33:43.620 --> 00:33:46.920
And the new node has the
old two nodes as children.

00:33:50.060 --> 00:33:55.968
The sum of the probabilities
of the two nodes,

00:33:55.968 --> 00:33:59.290
so 0.2 in this case.

00:33:59.290 --> 00:34:02.500
Now you have three
nodes left in the tree.

00:34:02.500 --> 00:34:05.140
You have A, B, and N1.

00:34:05.140 --> 00:34:07.870
You again take the
two lowest nodes.

00:34:07.870 --> 00:34:09.909
So now the two
lowest nodes are B,

00:34:09.909 --> 00:34:15.580
which is 0.25 and N1, which is
0.2, you merge those two nodes.

00:34:15.580 --> 00:34:17.560
So you merge them like this.

00:34:17.560 --> 00:34:20.139
You get another node
called N2, which

00:34:20.139 --> 00:34:26.380
has probability which is the sum
of 0.25 and 0.2, which is 0.45.

00:34:26.380 --> 00:34:28.060
Now you are just
left with two nodes,

00:34:28.060 --> 00:34:29.889
so they are the
two you will merge.

00:34:29.889 --> 00:34:33.429
You take A and N2,
you merge them.

00:34:33.429 --> 00:34:35.170
You get the root
node, let's call it

00:34:35.170 --> 00:34:36.760
N3, which has probability 1.

00:34:39.429 --> 00:34:41.120
And that's it basically.

00:34:41.120 --> 00:34:44.900
That that's the Huffman tree.

00:34:44.900 --> 00:34:46.780
And then you can--

00:34:46.780 --> 00:34:48.530
more of a afterthought,
but you can do it.

00:34:48.530 --> 00:34:52.115
You can just label these guys 1
0, and that gives you the code.

00:34:54.949 --> 00:34:58.370
So very simple construction.

00:34:58.370 --> 00:35:00.062
Any questions?

00:35:00.062 --> 00:35:01.520
Otherwise, we'll
do another example

00:35:01.520 --> 00:35:02.705
where you will help me out.

00:35:08.370 --> 00:35:12.330
Looks like we have [INAUDIBLE].

00:35:12.330 --> 00:35:12.830
Sorry.

00:35:17.290 --> 00:35:22.820
Huffman, this one will
be more interesting.

00:35:34.720 --> 00:35:38.020
Again, A, B, C,
D. This time there

00:35:38.020 --> 00:35:54.250
are five symbols, 0.3,
0.25, 0.2, 0.12, 0.08.

00:35:54.250 --> 00:36:01.150
So initially we have five nodes
in the tree, A, B, C, D, E.

00:36:01.150 --> 00:36:04.600
Somebody, which are the
first two nodes we merge,

00:36:04.600 --> 00:36:07.060
and what is the probability
of the merged node?

00:36:09.790 --> 00:36:13.270
So the answer is D and E.
And so let's merge D and E.

00:36:13.270 --> 00:36:15.820
And what is the probability of
this new node, let's call it

00:36:15.820 --> 00:36:16.320
N1?

00:36:16.320 --> 00:36:19.240
0.2.

00:36:19.240 --> 00:36:21.965
0.12 plus 0.08 is 0.2.

00:36:21.965 --> 00:36:22.465
Good.

00:36:25.670 --> 00:36:28.580
Now what are the next
two nodes we merge?

00:36:36.130 --> 00:36:39.190
Some people are whispering
very low voice please.

00:36:42.040 --> 00:36:43.310
Am I hearing C and N1?

00:36:48.940 --> 00:36:50.230
Let's merge C and N1.

00:36:52.830 --> 00:36:53.880
Let's call it N2.

00:36:53.880 --> 00:36:55.545
What is the probability of N2?

00:36:58.310 --> 00:37:02.690
0.4 because 0.2 plus 0.2 is 0.4.

00:37:02.690 --> 00:37:04.580
Next one.

00:37:04.580 --> 00:37:06.230
N2 and B.

00:37:06.230 --> 00:37:08.680
I heard N2 and B.
Anybody disagrees?

00:37:14.710 --> 00:37:17.670
Yeah.

00:37:17.670 --> 00:37:19.290
So now you have
three nodes left.

00:37:19.290 --> 00:37:20.760
You have N2, which is 0.4.

00:37:20.760 --> 00:37:22.290
You have B, which is 0.25.

00:37:22.290 --> 00:37:23.820
You have A, which is 0.35.

00:37:23.820 --> 00:37:26.730
The two lowest ones are
actually A and B at this point.

00:37:26.730 --> 00:37:32.270
So you merge A
and B. You get N3,

00:37:32.270 --> 00:37:38.540
which has probability 0.6
because 0.35 plus 0.25 is 0.6.

00:37:38.540 --> 00:37:42.170
And now you can just
merge these two guys,

00:37:42.170 --> 00:37:43.850
get the last root node.

00:37:43.850 --> 00:37:48.170
You get 1.0 and then
assign like this.

00:37:53.160 --> 00:37:55.020
Let's actually write
down the code words

00:37:55.020 --> 00:37:57.400
so that there is no
confusion at all.

00:37:57.400 --> 00:37:57.900
ABCDE.

00:38:02.520 --> 00:38:03.690
Codeword for A?

00:38:07.700 --> 00:38:08.420
00.

00:38:08.420 --> 00:38:09.275
Codeword for B?

00:38:12.490 --> 00:38:15.303
01 because we drew it
in a different way.

00:38:15.303 --> 00:38:16.720
The root is actually
on the right,

00:38:16.720 --> 00:38:18.310
so you need to go
from right to left.

00:38:18.310 --> 00:38:19.400
So B is 01.

00:38:19.400 --> 00:38:19.900
C?

00:38:25.260 --> 00:38:26.260
C is 10.

00:38:26.260 --> 00:38:26.760
D?

00:38:30.990 --> 00:38:32.610
Yeah, 110.

00:38:32.610 --> 00:38:37.170
And E is 111.

00:38:37.170 --> 00:38:40.470
We see it has the usual the
properties, the optimality

00:38:40.470 --> 00:38:42.930
properties we discussed
just now that the two

00:38:42.930 --> 00:38:45.750
longest codewords are D and E,
and they have the same length.

00:38:45.750 --> 00:38:46.740
That's good.

00:38:46.740 --> 00:38:50.070
And you can verify that if
one guy has higher probability

00:38:50.070 --> 00:38:53.400
than the other one, then
it has a equal to a smaller

00:38:53.400 --> 00:38:54.630
length than the other one.

00:38:54.630 --> 00:38:57.060
So it satisfies the
optimality properties.

00:39:03.410 --> 00:39:03.910
OK.

00:39:03.910 --> 00:39:07.240
Any questions about
the construction?

00:39:07.240 --> 00:39:13.560
Very simple construction,
really not that much to say.

00:39:16.500 --> 00:39:20.610
Try to implement it if you
want to do coding practice

00:39:20.610 --> 00:39:22.080
for some interviews or so on.

00:39:22.080 --> 00:39:24.510
It's a very classic
greedy algorithm.

00:39:24.510 --> 00:39:27.270
See what data structures you
use, do you use recursion,

00:39:27.270 --> 00:39:29.520
or do you use
iterative and so on.

00:39:41.170 --> 00:39:42.430
So why is it optimal?

00:39:42.430 --> 00:39:44.170
Just satisfying
those two conditions

00:39:44.170 --> 00:39:45.970
doesn't make the code optimal.

00:39:45.970 --> 00:39:49.030
You need to prove that
it is actually optimal.

00:39:49.030 --> 00:39:51.080
We will not prove it in class.

00:39:51.080 --> 00:39:54.370
So we will see
the lecture notes.

00:39:54.370 --> 00:39:56.620
Actually, it's not
in the lecture notes.

00:39:56.620 --> 00:39:58.420
You need to see the book.

00:39:58.420 --> 00:40:04.780
So you see Cover
and Thomas chapter 5

00:40:04.780 --> 00:40:07.750
for the proof of optimality.

00:40:07.750 --> 00:40:13.405
It's based on an argument
which is roughly that--

00:40:17.170 --> 00:40:27.540
let's see, ABCDE.

00:40:32.650 --> 00:40:36.970
So it's like an
inductive argument

00:40:36.970 --> 00:40:42.475
that optimality of this
guy is equivalent to--

00:40:55.110 --> 00:40:57.180
so it's a step by
step proof where

00:40:57.180 --> 00:41:01.440
you say that if my final code
is optimal, that's if and only

00:41:01.440 --> 00:41:04.470
if the code at my second--

00:41:04.470 --> 00:41:07.590
the second step is optimal,
which is optimal if

00:41:07.590 --> 00:41:10.530
and only if my code at
the third step is optimal.

00:41:10.530 --> 00:41:13.410
And at the very end you're
just left with two nodes

00:41:13.410 --> 00:41:15.810
because the last two
nodes are just 0 and 1.

00:41:15.810 --> 00:41:17.310
So that is definitely optimal.

00:41:17.310 --> 00:41:19.890
So it's that sort of inductive
proof where every step

00:41:19.890 --> 00:41:22.500
you show that this
step is optimal.

00:41:22.500 --> 00:41:26.640
And at the end, you get to the
base case which is optimal.

00:41:26.640 --> 00:41:27.780
We'll not do it.

00:41:27.780 --> 00:41:31.680
You don't need it
for the homeworks.

00:41:31.680 --> 00:41:32.730
It's interesting.

00:41:32.730 --> 00:41:34.380
Read about it.

00:41:34.380 --> 00:41:35.760
Greedy algorithms,
you will often

00:41:35.760 --> 00:41:38.520
see the proofs are
sometimes a bit tricky.

00:41:38.520 --> 00:41:39.660
This is one of them where--

00:41:39.660 --> 00:41:42.810
you need some amount of
math writing to prove it.

00:41:42.810 --> 00:41:45.015
But fundamentally,
this is the idea.

00:41:56.152 --> 00:41:58.110
So the first thing, as
you might have observed,

00:41:58.110 --> 00:41:59.400
it's a greedy algorithm.

00:41:59.400 --> 00:42:02.457
We don't try to do some
sort of global optimization.

00:42:02.457 --> 00:42:05.040
At every step, we just take the
two smallest things, merge two

00:42:05.040 --> 00:42:07.960
smallest things, merge so on.

00:42:07.960 --> 00:42:11.130
So that's something.

00:42:11.130 --> 00:42:21.960
Works for general Wi is
greater than or equal to 0

00:42:21.960 --> 00:42:24.060
to solve this sort of problem.

00:42:24.060 --> 00:42:31.060
If you have any weighted
optimization problem,

00:42:31.060 --> 00:42:33.060
then it not be probabilities
is what I'm saying.

00:42:33.060 --> 00:42:38.160
You can take any W's, and
it's the same algorithm.

00:42:38.160 --> 00:42:39.078
It doesn't matter.

00:42:39.078 --> 00:42:41.370
So sometimes you will see
applications of Huffman codes

00:42:41.370 --> 00:42:43.020
beyond just coding.

00:42:43.020 --> 00:42:46.260
In some other areas, you
might see some of them

00:42:46.260 --> 00:42:48.180
in either this or
the next homework.

00:42:53.200 --> 00:43:02.620
And one last point is,
this will be very important

00:43:02.620 --> 00:43:07.270
for this homework, so just be
very careful, the tie breaking.

00:43:07.270 --> 00:43:10.570
We said that if you have
two possibilities then--

00:43:10.570 --> 00:43:12.130
if you have three
nodes, each of them

00:43:12.130 --> 00:43:14.428
have 0.2, 0.2,
0.2, you can either

00:43:14.428 --> 00:43:15.970
merge the first two
or the second two

00:43:15.970 --> 00:43:17.350
or the first and the third.

00:43:17.350 --> 00:43:20.800
So there is a
non-determinism built in.

00:43:20.800 --> 00:43:22.780
The construction is
not deterministic.

00:43:22.780 --> 00:43:25.810
In many cases, you can have
more than one possible optimal

00:43:25.810 --> 00:43:26.950
Huffman code.

00:43:26.950 --> 00:43:30.970
So when you do the homework
problem 5, be very careful.

00:43:30.970 --> 00:43:34.240
Last year lots of students
have a lot of issues.

00:43:34.240 --> 00:43:39.160
So just be careful that the
tie breaking step in particular

00:43:39.160 --> 00:43:49.350
means that you have multiple
possible Huffman codes.

00:43:49.350 --> 00:43:53.940
All of them will have the
same expected code length.

00:43:53.940 --> 00:43:57.270
All of them are optimal,
but they are different.

00:44:09.020 --> 00:44:11.990
Let me go back actually here.

00:44:11.990 --> 00:44:12.490
No.

00:44:15.580 --> 00:44:17.500
Last thing is that
for Shannon codes

00:44:17.500 --> 00:44:22.150
we saw that expected code length
is between Hx and Hx plus 1.

00:44:22.150 --> 00:44:25.630
For Huffman codes, that's
basically what we have.

00:44:25.630 --> 00:44:29.710
We know-- maybe let
me write it like this.

00:44:29.710 --> 00:44:34.740
So we know that Hx greater than
or equal to expected length

00:44:34.740 --> 00:44:39.120
of Huffman codes,
which is less than

00:44:39.120 --> 00:44:47.600
or equal to expected length
of Shannon codes, which

00:44:47.600 --> 00:44:50.240
is less than Hx plus 1.

00:44:50.240 --> 00:44:52.620
So this is the chain
of inequalities.

00:44:52.620 --> 00:44:55.160
So Huffman codes are somewhere
between Shannon codes

00:44:55.160 --> 00:44:58.100
and entropy.

00:44:58.100 --> 00:44:59.750
For certain types
of distributions,

00:44:59.750 --> 00:45:01.550
you can prove stronger
results but really

00:45:01.550 --> 00:45:03.305
this is what you have.

00:45:13.200 --> 00:45:24.180
Coming back to the
construction, can somebody

00:45:24.180 --> 00:45:26.190
suggest how do I do this step?

00:45:26.190 --> 00:45:27.870
How do I always
pick the two nodes

00:45:27.870 --> 00:45:30.570
with the least probability?

00:45:30.570 --> 00:45:33.630
Can I do it in an
efficient way, maybe even

00:45:33.630 --> 00:45:35.730
in an efficient
way, just any ideas

00:45:35.730 --> 00:45:39.330
on how we can pick the two nodes
with the least probability?

00:45:39.330 --> 00:45:41.370
How would you do it if
you were implementing it

00:45:41.370 --> 00:45:42.530
in Python, let's say?

00:45:50.880 --> 00:45:53.960
We could do the heaps or
priority queues to do this.

00:45:53.960 --> 00:45:54.980
Yeah.

00:45:54.980 --> 00:45:58.970
Correctly suggests that you
could use a heap or a priority

00:45:58.970 --> 00:46:02.570
queue, which is a data structure
where you can always pull out

00:46:02.570 --> 00:46:05.600
the lowest thing or
the highest thing

00:46:05.600 --> 00:46:09.530
depending on the type of heap
or priority queue you're using.

00:46:09.530 --> 00:46:11.540
If you're not seeing
those, it's fine.

00:46:11.540 --> 00:46:14.930
Just you should be aware.

00:46:14.930 --> 00:46:16.400
There is an inefficient way.

00:46:16.400 --> 00:46:18.230
You take the list of nodes.

00:46:18.230 --> 00:46:19.100
You sort it.

00:46:19.100 --> 00:46:20.840
You take the two lowest ones.

00:46:20.840 --> 00:46:23.990
Then you add the new
node, the N1 node.

00:46:23.990 --> 00:46:25.670
And then you again
sort it, and then

00:46:25.670 --> 00:46:27.260
you again take the two lowest.

00:46:27.260 --> 00:46:28.160
That also works.

00:46:28.160 --> 00:46:31.370
It's less efficient,
but it works.

00:46:31.370 --> 00:46:35.270
Heaps or priority queues
are, I guess, easier.

00:46:35.270 --> 00:46:37.730
Another thing to note is
that in many situations,

00:46:37.730 --> 00:46:40.070
you will construct the
Huffman code once and use it

00:46:40.070 --> 00:46:41.240
multiple times.

00:46:41.240 --> 00:46:44.390
So it's fine if the
construction is a bit slow

00:46:44.390 --> 00:46:46.700
because ultimately, you're
going to construct it once

00:46:46.700 --> 00:46:49.640
for a distribution and then keep
using it for lots of symbols.

00:46:56.250 --> 00:47:00.960
Let's actually look at
a code for constructing

00:47:00.960 --> 00:47:04.380
this in the library.

00:47:08.900 --> 00:47:13.330
Let me know if I need
to zoom at some point.

00:47:13.330 --> 00:47:14.500
So we go to SCL.

00:47:14.500 --> 00:47:15.940
We go to compressors.

00:47:15.940 --> 00:47:17.820
We go to Huffman_coder.

00:47:23.378 --> 00:47:24.420
I'll walk you through it.

00:47:24.420 --> 00:47:27.480
The file is this one,
stanford_compression_library,

00:47:27.480 --> 00:47:30.750
scl, compressors,
huffman_coder.py.

00:47:30.750 --> 00:47:32.865
That's the file if you
want to later look.

00:47:35.730 --> 00:47:39.000
So what we do is we define
this thing called a HuffmanNode

00:47:39.000 --> 00:47:42.810
where the HuffmanNode has
a less than or equal to

00:47:42.810 --> 00:47:46.740
implementation, which just
says self.prob less than

00:47:46.740 --> 00:47:48.360
or equal to other.prob.

00:47:48.360 --> 00:47:51.630
So basically, what
we are saying is

00:47:51.630 --> 00:47:55.350
you are creating this
class in Python, which

00:47:55.350 --> 00:47:57.198
has this ordering property.

00:47:57.198 --> 00:47:59.490
You can define which node is
bigger than the other node

00:47:59.490 --> 00:48:02.040
based on their probabilities.

00:48:02.040 --> 00:48:05.880
So then there is a function
called build_huffman_tree,

00:48:05.880 --> 00:48:08.260
which is what we
were just doing.

00:48:08.260 --> 00:48:11.220
So let's see what
the function does.

00:48:11.220 --> 00:48:13.350
If the length of
the alphabet is 1,

00:48:13.350 --> 00:48:16.200
so if you are at the last node
or there is just one node,

00:48:16.200 --> 00:48:19.120
there is nothing to do.

00:48:19.120 --> 00:48:24.370
You just-- that's the base
case, I guess, of the recursion.

00:48:24.370 --> 00:48:26.080
What else?

00:48:26.080 --> 00:48:31.500
You start with a node_list equal
to the empty list, so I'm here.

00:48:31.500 --> 00:48:33.000
Yeah.

00:48:33.000 --> 00:48:39.650
Then for a in self.alphabet,
you create these nodes

00:48:39.650 --> 00:48:42.680
and you append them
to the node_list.

00:48:42.680 --> 00:48:46.640
And then you use this function
called heapify, which converts

00:48:46.640 --> 00:48:47.780
this thing into a heap.

00:48:50.390 --> 00:48:54.680
And then you have a loop where
while the number of nodes

00:48:54.680 --> 00:48:59.640
in your heap is more than 1,
you first heapify the thing,

00:48:59.640 --> 00:49:02.070
then you take the two
smallest symbols, very easy.

00:49:02.070 --> 00:49:05.670
You pop from the heap.

00:49:05.670 --> 00:49:08.670
And then you create
this combined symbol

00:49:08.670 --> 00:49:10.200
which we were just
talking about,

00:49:10.200 --> 00:49:12.780
so the last1.pro and last2.prob.

00:49:12.780 --> 00:49:18.150
You create a combined node, and
you push it back into the heap.

00:49:18.150 --> 00:49:20.490
And at the end, you're left
with a single node, which

00:49:20.490 --> 00:49:22.155
is the root node of the thing.

00:49:24.983 --> 00:49:27.650
So basically the construction we
saw you can very easily code it

00:49:27.650 --> 00:49:29.630
up in like less than--

00:49:29.630 --> 00:49:31.700
if you remove the comments,
it's less than 10,

00:49:31.700 --> 00:49:36.890
15 lines of Python
really, so very simple.

00:49:36.890 --> 00:49:38.495
And any questions on this?

00:49:51.570 --> 00:49:53.730
Yeah.

00:49:53.730 --> 00:49:55.980
So depending on
whether you find--

00:49:55.980 --> 00:49:59.550
you learn easily with code
or with the pseudo code,

00:49:59.550 --> 00:50:01.440
you have both options available.

00:50:16.040 --> 00:50:19.370
So one topic which again
connects to the homework

00:50:19.370 --> 00:50:23.240
question 4 is decoding.

00:50:28.370 --> 00:50:30.740
So if you remember
we, in lecture 2,

00:50:30.740 --> 00:50:33.620
actually we talked about you
have this prefix codes that

00:50:33.620 --> 00:50:35.060
can be represented with trees.

00:50:35.060 --> 00:50:37.190
So the decoding is so easy.

00:50:37.190 --> 00:50:38.390
You just walk down the tree.

00:50:38.390 --> 00:50:40.940
You decode a symbol.

00:50:40.940 --> 00:50:42.350
We were very happy.

00:50:42.350 --> 00:50:45.710
So we talked about this
tree-based decoding

00:50:45.710 --> 00:50:49.750
where the idea was
very simple really.

00:50:49.750 --> 00:50:56.050
You have this tree,
like this, 0110.

00:50:56.050 --> 00:51:02.440
And then you had a stream which
was like 01011 sort of thing.

00:51:02.440 --> 00:51:07.090
And then you would go here,
then you would go like this,

00:51:07.090 --> 00:51:09.670
then you would go like this,
and then you would decode it.

00:51:13.960 --> 00:51:16.165
Sadly, nobody uses this.

00:51:16.165 --> 00:51:20.470
This is not a good
idea in practice.

00:51:20.470 --> 00:51:21.730
Two reasons.

00:51:21.730 --> 00:51:26.870
One is, there are
too many branches.

00:51:26.870 --> 00:51:28.280
Branches in the
sense that if you

00:51:28.280 --> 00:51:31.190
write the Python code for this,
there is if-else statements.

00:51:31.190 --> 00:51:32.390
If this is 1, go left.

00:51:32.390 --> 00:51:36.440
If this is 0, go
right, that branches.

00:51:36.440 --> 00:51:37.760
So this sort of thing.

00:51:37.760 --> 00:51:40.310
If 0, left.

00:51:40.310 --> 00:51:43.730
If 1, right.

00:51:43.730 --> 00:51:46.820
And branches are
really, really bad

00:51:46.820 --> 00:51:54.860
for modern computers, modern
computer architectures.

00:51:59.440 --> 00:52:01.630
We won't do a computer
architecture course right now

00:52:01.630 --> 00:52:05.770
but in short the reason is they
do something called pipelining

00:52:05.770 --> 00:52:09.280
where they collect a
bunch of instructions

00:52:09.280 --> 00:52:11.740
and try to execute them
in a pipelined way.

00:52:11.740 --> 00:52:13.270
And if you take
the wrong branch,

00:52:13.270 --> 00:52:15.100
then you have to flush
the whole pipeline.

00:52:15.100 --> 00:52:16.780
A lot of work goes to waste.

00:52:16.780 --> 00:52:18.160
So it's just bad.

00:52:18.160 --> 00:52:19.657
Branches are bad.

00:52:19.657 --> 00:52:21.490
Even though you have
good branch predictors,

00:52:21.490 --> 00:52:25.030
still branches make
your code really slow.

00:52:25.030 --> 00:52:27.760
And you might wonder
like, why do we care?

00:52:27.760 --> 00:52:30.610
Do we really decode so
much data with Huffman?

00:52:30.610 --> 00:52:31.750
Actually, we do.

00:52:31.750 --> 00:52:35.170
Gzip, one of the most
popular compression formats,

00:52:35.170 --> 00:52:37.570
uses Huffman as
its entropy coder.

00:52:37.570 --> 00:52:41.080
And every time you download
a file from the internet,

00:52:41.080 --> 00:52:43.630
there is probably a
Huffman coder running.

00:52:43.630 --> 00:52:46.000
The older video codecs
used to use Huffman.

00:52:46.000 --> 00:52:49.210
The new ones I don't know.

00:52:49.210 --> 00:52:51.550
Every time you're streaming
a video over YouTube,

00:52:51.550 --> 00:52:54.130
either this or something
similar is actually running.

00:52:54.130 --> 00:52:55.875
You're always decoding
a prefix code.

00:52:55.875 --> 00:52:57.250
Anytime your
computer is running,

00:52:57.250 --> 00:53:00.830
you're probably decoding
a prefix code somewhere.

00:53:00.830 --> 00:53:03.230
So this is not
just about Huffman.

00:53:03.230 --> 00:53:06.200
This is about any [INAUDIBLE]
just this tree-based decoding

00:53:06.200 --> 00:53:11.150
is a really, really bad idea
because of these branches.

00:53:11.150 --> 00:53:13.310
You will never get the
same video throughput

00:53:13.310 --> 00:53:15.800
that you get today.

00:53:15.800 --> 00:53:19.880
So let's look at
an alternate way.

00:53:19.880 --> 00:53:23.930
We'll look at it briefly now
and then later in the homework,

00:53:23.930 --> 00:53:27.850
you will actually
implement the decoding.

00:53:27.850 --> 00:53:33.570
And this is question
4 of the homework.

00:53:37.690 --> 00:53:38.190
Yeah.

00:53:38.190 --> 00:53:40.232
You implement it in the
context of Shannon codes,

00:53:40.232 --> 00:53:43.110
but the idea is the same.

00:53:43.110 --> 00:53:49.800
So I will briefly
describe it so that you

00:53:49.800 --> 00:53:53.820
have some context when you
start the homework question.

00:53:53.820 --> 00:53:57.840
So there is a decoder
state table, which is 000--

00:53:57.840 --> 00:54:01.630
actually, let me draw code here.

00:54:01.630 --> 00:54:03.970
So we draw this code A is 0.

00:54:03.970 --> 00:54:05.350
B is 10.

00:54:05.350 --> 00:54:06.820
C is 110.

00:54:06.820 --> 00:54:38.750
D is 111.

00:54:38.750 --> 00:54:42.780
so this is the code state table.

00:54:42.780 --> 00:54:44.550
And then you have
another table which

00:54:44.550 --> 00:54:54.475
is called encode-len,
which is ABCD, 1, 2, 3, 3.

00:55:00.400 --> 00:55:05.980
Anybody wants to guess what the
decode stage table is doing?

00:55:11.670 --> 00:55:13.260
This is all
corresponding to the code

00:55:13.260 --> 00:55:18.575
I made on the right, the
A, 0, B, 10, that one.

00:55:18.575 --> 00:55:22.100
We basically just grab three
bits at a time of a sequence

00:55:22.100 --> 00:55:25.420
and then check which
symbol it corresponds to

00:55:25.420 --> 00:55:27.640
in the decode_state_table.

00:55:27.640 --> 00:55:31.980
From there, we can advance
forward based on code lengths.

00:55:31.980 --> 00:55:35.570
Not all of our symbols
are being decoded.

00:55:35.570 --> 00:55:36.070
Yeah.

00:55:36.070 --> 00:55:38.080
So this was basically
the full answer.

00:55:38.080 --> 00:55:41.470
So let's start step by step.

00:55:41.470 --> 00:55:43.060
So what does it mean that--

00:55:43.060 --> 00:55:46.600
somebody else, what does it
mean that 011 is decoded to A,

00:55:46.600 --> 00:55:47.500
what does that mean?

00:55:52.320 --> 00:55:55.245
Why are there like four
things, 000 is recorded to A,

00:55:55.245 --> 00:55:57.540
001 decoded to A, 010011?

00:55:57.540 --> 00:55:59.850
Why are all four decoded to A?

00:55:59.850 --> 00:56:00.720
Just try to guess.

00:56:00.720 --> 00:56:03.570
I haven't obviously told
you what we are doing,

00:56:03.570 --> 00:56:04.830
but if you had to guess.

00:56:08.710 --> 00:56:16.530
[INAUDIBLE] is A, like 0,
that can be encoded to A.

00:56:16.530 --> 00:56:17.580
Yeah.

00:56:17.580 --> 00:56:19.800
So then you look at the prefix.

00:56:19.800 --> 00:56:22.110
Just the way we were
doing the prefix coding--

00:56:22.110 --> 00:56:25.800
prefix decoding in the
past, you look at a sequence

00:56:25.800 --> 00:56:28.437
and you try to find
the first symbol

00:56:28.437 --> 00:56:29.520
that you decode out of it.

00:56:29.520 --> 00:56:32.880
So if you see a 000, the first
symbol you would decode out

00:56:32.880 --> 00:56:34.800
of it is A. It starts with zero.

00:56:34.800 --> 00:56:37.600
The only codeword
that starts with zero.

00:56:37.600 --> 00:56:39.940
If you see a 100--

00:56:39.940 --> 00:56:43.960
if you see a 100 or a 101, the
first symbol you would decode

00:56:43.960 --> 00:56:47.860
is a B because the B is the
only one that starts with 10.

00:56:47.860 --> 00:56:51.310
If you see a 110, then you would
decode a C. If you see a 111,

00:56:51.310 --> 00:57:02.140
you would decode a D. So with
this in mind, you can define--

00:57:02.140 --> 00:57:04.690
you can implement this function.

00:57:07.330 --> 00:57:09.583
I think this is
actually not what you're

00:57:09.583 --> 00:57:10.750
asked to do in the homework.

00:57:10.750 --> 00:57:13.690
You are asked to construct these
state tables in the homework.

00:57:13.690 --> 00:57:18.610
So I'm not infringing
upon your homework here.

00:57:18.610 --> 00:57:22.330
So we call it
decode_symbol_fast,

00:57:22.330 --> 00:57:26.860
fast in the sense that it's
faster than doing it in a tree.

00:57:26.860 --> 00:57:30.170
So you define this
state, which is,

00:57:30.170 --> 00:57:33.260
you take the three bits
from the bit array--

00:57:33.260 --> 00:57:36.500
the next three bits
from the bit array.

00:57:36.500 --> 00:57:45.180
You look at the
decode state table,

00:57:45.180 --> 00:57:47.670
and you can decode
a symbol already.

00:57:47.670 --> 00:57:48.690
You can decode that.

00:57:48.690 --> 00:57:50.985
Based on the three bits,
you can decode the symbol.

00:57:54.220 --> 00:57:56.993
And then you look at the
encode_len table, which tells

00:57:56.993 --> 00:57:58.285
you the length of the encoding.

00:58:09.130 --> 00:58:11.710
And then you can
basically return

00:58:11.710 --> 00:58:13.135
S and the number of bits.

00:58:18.510 --> 00:58:23.360
So it's a few steps
of things to do.

00:58:23.360 --> 00:58:27.470
First thing is you take the next
three bits from the bit area.

00:58:27.470 --> 00:58:30.440
Based on those three bits,
you decode one symbol, only

00:58:30.440 --> 00:58:31.430
one symbol.

00:58:31.430 --> 00:58:35.320
So you can decode any
of these ABCD symbols.

00:58:35.320 --> 00:58:37.660
Then based on the symbol
you decode you check,

00:58:37.660 --> 00:58:40.240
how many bits do I actually
need, how many bits

00:58:40.240 --> 00:58:42.320
I have actually read?

00:58:42.320 --> 00:58:44.750
And then you basically advance.

00:58:44.750 --> 00:58:46.760
So that's the process.

00:58:46.760 --> 00:58:48.410
And you will get
much more practice

00:58:48.410 --> 00:58:49.493
with this in the homework.

00:58:49.493 --> 00:58:53.120
But here I just wanted
to give you a flavor.

00:58:53.120 --> 00:58:55.018
Any questions on this?

00:58:55.018 --> 00:58:56.810
And if there are no
questions, can somebody

00:58:56.810 --> 00:58:58.340
tell me what this three is?

00:58:58.340 --> 00:58:59.270
Why three?

00:58:59.270 --> 00:59:01.760
What's so special about three?

00:59:01.760 --> 00:59:03.940
It's the longest.

00:59:03.940 --> 00:59:08.870
Yeah, I hear that three is
the longest codeword here.

00:59:08.870 --> 00:59:09.370
Yeah.

00:59:09.370 --> 00:59:10.620
I have a question.

00:59:10.620 --> 00:59:11.800
Yep.

00:59:11.800 --> 00:59:14.230
What situation would
we have this bit array

00:59:14.230 --> 00:59:17.920
with symbols of three when we're
dealing with the prefix code?

00:59:17.920 --> 00:59:19.900
It just seems--

00:59:19.900 --> 00:59:21.070
Yeah.

00:59:21.070 --> 00:59:23.200
So the way to think
about it is that you are

00:59:23.200 --> 00:59:25.420
given a long, long sequence.

00:59:25.420 --> 00:59:28.900
It's just a stream coming in
or a big block or something.

00:59:28.900 --> 00:59:31.330
You have encoded a
bunch of ABCDs together.

00:59:31.330 --> 00:59:33.190
You got a long bit array.

00:59:33.190 --> 00:59:36.940
And then you take
the first three bits.

00:59:36.940 --> 00:59:39.490
Based on that, you
decode one symbol,

00:59:39.490 --> 00:59:41.860
and you know that you
have read maybe one bit.

00:59:41.860 --> 00:59:44.380
Maybe it was an A, so
you only read one bit.

00:59:44.380 --> 00:59:46.270
So next time, you will look at--

00:59:46.270 --> 00:59:49.210
if the first time you
look at bit 1, 2, 3,

00:59:49.210 --> 00:59:51.610
next time you look
at bit 2, 3, 4.

00:59:51.610 --> 00:59:54.130
And then maybe you
read two symbols.

00:59:54.130 --> 00:59:56.830
So now you have consumed
bits 2 and bits 3.

00:59:56.830 --> 00:59:58.780
So now you look at 4, 5, 6.

00:59:58.780 --> 01:00:00.460
So that's the progression.

01:00:00.460 --> 01:00:02.950
It's just not clear to me
why that array would even

01:00:02.950 --> 01:00:04.320
exist in first place.

01:00:04.320 --> 01:00:06.520
What is the--

01:00:06.520 --> 01:00:07.020
Yeah.

01:00:12.430 --> 01:00:12.930
Yeah.

01:00:12.930 --> 01:00:15.060
I think we talked about
it a little bit earlier.

01:00:15.060 --> 01:00:17.880
I think as you do the homework,
you will get more experience.

01:00:17.880 --> 01:00:21.060
But the whole reason
why we talk about all

01:00:21.060 --> 01:00:24.300
this prefix free codes, why
do we need prefix free codes,

01:00:24.300 --> 01:00:27.090
why is it not sufficient that
ABCD just have different code

01:00:27.090 --> 01:00:31.080
words, why the prefix free
property is because almost all

01:00:31.080 --> 01:00:33.750
the time, you don't want
to encode just one symbol.

01:00:33.750 --> 01:00:37.530
You want to encode a
long string of symbols.

01:00:37.530 --> 01:00:41.760
So the thing you encode
is like ACDBBDD-something.

01:00:41.760 --> 01:00:45.870
And that whole big thing will be
converted to a long bit array,

01:00:45.870 --> 01:00:48.450
like 0011010 whatever.

01:00:48.450 --> 01:00:50.160
So you get this long thing.

01:00:50.160 --> 01:00:51.780
And then that long
thing, you should

01:00:51.780 --> 01:00:55.500
be able to decode back
to the original sequence.

01:00:55.500 --> 01:00:56.730
Yeah.

01:00:56.730 --> 01:00:58.180
Look at the lecture
2 slides where

01:00:58.180 --> 01:01:00.180
we talked about-- there
is an alternate approach

01:01:00.180 --> 01:01:01.597
in a Morse code
or something where

01:01:01.597 --> 01:01:03.900
you put a comma or a separator
between every symbol,

01:01:03.900 --> 01:01:06.300
but that's not how
we do it typically.

01:01:06.300 --> 01:01:07.590
It's not optimal.

01:01:07.590 --> 01:01:09.090
So it's better to do--

01:01:09.090 --> 01:01:13.230
have it prefix-free so that
you can encode a long sequence

01:01:13.230 --> 01:01:15.630
into a long bit array.

01:01:15.630 --> 01:01:18.840
So that's how you get
the three basically.

01:01:18.840 --> 01:01:21.835
At the very end, you might
reach the very end of the thing

01:01:21.835 --> 01:01:24.210
where maybe you don't have--
it's not a multiple of three

01:01:24.210 --> 01:01:26.160
or there is some
boundary conditions.

01:01:26.160 --> 01:01:30.630
So you can pad it with zeros
just to make sure it works out.

01:01:30.630 --> 01:01:31.320
Pulkit, yeah.

01:01:31.320 --> 01:01:33.690
I wanted to just
clarify the last point

01:01:33.690 --> 01:01:37.260
that sure there may be some
edge cases boundary condition.

01:01:37.260 --> 01:01:41.020
So suppose you are encoding
AB010 in that case,

01:01:41.020 --> 01:01:42.930
you read three,
you decode it say,

01:01:42.930 --> 01:01:45.300
you can only decode one symbol.

01:01:45.300 --> 01:01:46.740
You are still left
with one zero.

01:01:46.740 --> 01:01:48.090
But that's just a corner case.

01:01:48.090 --> 01:01:51.270
You can just always
append last bit as zero

01:01:51.270 --> 01:01:55.930
if it's not a multiple of three,
read 100 and that's B again.

01:01:55.930 --> 01:01:57.800
That's where you stop decode.

01:01:57.800 --> 01:01:58.300
Yeah.

01:01:58.300 --> 01:01:59.800
So Pulkit just
talking about padding

01:01:59.800 --> 01:02:02.620
basically that at the
end, you might not

01:02:02.620 --> 01:02:05.230
be left with three symbols when
you decode a long sequence.

01:02:05.230 --> 01:02:06.450
You just pad it with zeros.

01:02:06.450 --> 01:02:07.075
It's fine.

01:02:09.820 --> 01:02:11.020
Any other questions?

01:02:17.180 --> 01:02:17.680
Sorry.

01:02:23.880 --> 01:02:26.190
So one more thing about--

01:02:26.190 --> 01:02:33.220
a couple more things about
table-based decoding,

01:02:33.220 --> 01:02:38.680
so the size of the
table is exponential in

01:02:38.680 --> 01:02:42.250
the maximum depth of the tree.

01:02:42.250 --> 01:02:44.710
We saw that in the last one
the table size was eight

01:02:44.710 --> 01:02:48.270
because 2 power 3 is 8.

01:02:48.270 --> 01:02:50.340
And what you want
is in practice,

01:02:50.340 --> 01:02:53.610
you want it to
fit in your cache.

01:02:53.610 --> 01:02:55.830
So it should be very small
so that your decoding

01:02:55.830 --> 01:02:58.020
can work very fast.

01:02:58.020 --> 01:03:04.270
So therefore, you want
to limit max depth.

01:03:09.760 --> 01:03:12.430
So this is something we
will not do in class.

01:03:12.430 --> 01:03:14.890
Last year, a couple students
did it actually as a project.

01:03:14.890 --> 01:03:20.890
But there is something
called a constrained Huffman

01:03:20.890 --> 01:03:35.620
code where the idea is best
code with max depth constraint.

01:03:41.855 --> 01:03:43.730
The construction is
slightly more complicated

01:03:43.730 --> 01:03:45.650
than your usual Huffman
codes, but these

01:03:45.650 --> 01:03:48.260
are the version of Huffman
codes that you will often

01:03:48.260 --> 01:03:50.258
see being used in practice.

01:03:50.258 --> 01:03:51.800
You want to constrain
the max length.

01:03:51.800 --> 01:03:54.080
And often you want
to constrain it

01:03:54.080 --> 01:03:59.570
to 16 or 24 or a
byte multiple thing

01:03:59.570 --> 01:04:02.525
so that it works nicely
with how computers work.

01:04:06.380 --> 01:04:07.570
Any questions on this?

01:04:10.260 --> 01:04:10.760
Yeah.

01:04:10.760 --> 01:04:15.260
So this is really
low level stuff.

01:04:15.260 --> 01:04:17.420
But you will see a lot
of action and compression

01:04:17.420 --> 01:04:19.040
is often in the very
low level stuff.

01:04:19.040 --> 01:04:21.080
You need to be-- you
have a good algorithm.

01:04:21.080 --> 01:04:21.860
That's good.

01:04:21.860 --> 01:04:25.220
But if it uses all these
complicated trees and so on,

01:04:25.220 --> 01:04:27.950
you can't use it for the
large volumes of data.

01:04:27.950 --> 01:04:29.210
Videos are huge.

01:04:29.210 --> 01:04:30.680
Logs are huge.

01:04:30.680 --> 01:04:32.930
Text is not that big.

01:04:32.930 --> 01:04:36.110
So if you're just
compressing text, go all in.

01:04:36.110 --> 01:04:38.090
But for practical
types of data, it's

01:04:38.090 --> 01:04:40.640
often so big that
you can't afford

01:04:40.640 --> 01:04:42.545
suboptimal types of things.

01:04:46.450 --> 01:04:49.330
Last thing-- on table-based
coding the last thing--

01:04:52.040 --> 01:04:57.730
table-based decoding,
more of a homework really,

01:04:57.730 --> 01:04:59.935
does anybody recognize
this sequence?

01:05:11.200 --> 01:05:13.120
Yeah, some people are
correctly answering.

01:05:13.120 --> 01:05:14.335
It's the Fibonacci sequence.

01:05:17.850 --> 01:05:19.620
What is the sum of 1 plus 1, 2.

01:05:19.620 --> 01:05:21.180
2 plus 2, 4.

01:05:21.180 --> 01:05:23.670
4 plus 3 is 7.

01:05:23.670 --> 01:05:25.780
12.

01:05:25.780 --> 01:05:28.840
20, 33.

01:05:28.840 --> 01:05:31.360
So we make this
probability distribution.

01:05:35.662 --> 01:05:40.290
We make this probability
distribution.

01:05:40.290 --> 01:05:43.545
Just make the Huffman
code for this at home.

01:05:48.970 --> 01:05:50.590
What you will
observe is that this

01:05:50.590 --> 01:05:53.710
is an adversarial case where
you will get really, really

01:05:53.710 --> 01:05:55.390
long codewords.

01:05:55.390 --> 01:05:57.610
You might think that why
worry about this max depth?

01:05:57.610 --> 01:06:00.010
In practice, you will never
have a very long code word.

01:06:00.010 --> 01:06:01.990
All the code words will
be short and so on.

01:06:01.990 --> 01:06:03.910
But there are these
special distributions

01:06:03.910 --> 01:06:08.830
where you will see that
the max depth in this case

01:06:08.830 --> 01:06:14.140
will be basically equal to
the number of symbols, that

01:06:14.140 --> 01:06:16.480
minus 1, something like that.

01:06:16.480 --> 01:06:19.300
So the max depth will be roughly
equal to the number of symbols.

01:06:19.300 --> 01:06:22.750
So you will get a really, really
long codeword, like 111111111

01:06:22.750 --> 01:06:24.590
so on.

01:06:24.590 --> 01:06:28.122
So solve it at home.

01:06:28.122 --> 01:06:29.080
Hopefully, you enjoyed.

01:06:33.170 --> 01:06:39.220
Moving on to some practical
Huffman codes, so first thing,

01:06:39.220 --> 01:06:41.860
this is like somebody
did a nice experiment

01:06:41.860 --> 01:06:45.490
where they took a
keyboard and the height

01:06:45.490 --> 01:06:47.440
of the letters on
the keyboard are

01:06:47.440 --> 01:06:49.810
the frequency of that symbol.

01:06:49.810 --> 01:06:51.580
So E is very, very high.

01:06:51.580 --> 01:06:52.990
T is the second highest.

01:06:52.990 --> 01:06:57.130
A is the third highest,
so just in English.

01:06:57.130 --> 01:06:59.560
And then they also
made this Huffman code

01:06:59.560 --> 01:07:01.300
for this distribution.

01:07:01.300 --> 01:07:07.138
And you can see at the top
part for example that--

01:07:07.138 --> 01:07:07.638
sorry.

01:07:21.440 --> 01:07:21.980
Yeah.

01:07:21.980 --> 01:07:23.750
So E you see--

01:07:23.750 --> 01:07:26.210
E is very short codeword.

01:07:26.210 --> 01:07:30.260
Z, Q, X, J, the less commonly
used letters in English

01:07:30.260 --> 01:07:33.230
are much longer.

01:07:33.230 --> 01:07:36.590
But it's non-trivial, if you
didn't know the algorithm,

01:07:36.590 --> 01:07:40.190
this is the optimal code for
this distribution, so just

01:07:40.190 --> 01:07:40.940
something.

01:07:45.340 --> 01:07:51.960
At home, I did a
few more using SCL.

01:07:56.200 --> 01:08:00.670
So I followed basically
Pulkit's tutorial of SCL.

01:08:00.670 --> 01:08:03.850
I installed SCL.

01:08:03.850 --> 01:08:06.580
I downloaded this
very famous book,

01:08:06.580 --> 01:08:12.797
which I guess you know,
Huckleberry Finn by Mark Twain.

01:08:12.797 --> 01:08:14.170
Can you make the size big.

01:08:14.170 --> 01:08:16.000
Oh, yeah, let me
make it a bit bigger.

01:08:16.000 --> 01:08:17.710
Yeah.

01:08:17.710 --> 01:08:20.020
So we're saying this
book, normal English text

01:08:20.020 --> 01:08:21.444
about treasures and so on.

01:08:24.910 --> 01:08:28.930
And then I used SCL to
create the Huffman tree

01:08:28.930 --> 01:08:30.580
for this distribution.

01:08:37.200 --> 01:08:38.640
We will post this.

01:08:38.640 --> 01:08:39.240
Take a look.

01:08:39.240 --> 01:08:44.130
But you see the letter small
e is very short codeword.

01:08:44.130 --> 01:08:48.899
Letter capital E, uppercase
E, is much less common

01:08:48.899 --> 01:08:51.630
because E is very common,
but E doesn't often

01:08:51.630 --> 01:08:55.439
come at the start of
a sentence actually.

01:09:00.740 --> 01:09:01.240
OK.

01:09:01.240 --> 01:09:03.420
We'll try to scroll down.

01:09:03.420 --> 01:09:05.685
Then what I did was
I actually used--

01:09:08.590 --> 01:09:09.130
sorry.

01:09:09.130 --> 01:09:12.540
This is just hard to do--

01:09:12.540 --> 01:09:14.084
is it visible?

01:09:14.084 --> 01:09:14.584
No.

01:09:22.149 --> 01:09:22.720
Yeah.

01:09:22.720 --> 01:09:25.689
So I actually compressed
it with the Huffman code.

01:09:25.689 --> 01:09:30.430
So I started with a 622
kilobyte file, 622,542.

01:09:30.430 --> 01:09:33.189
It got compressed to 348,142.

01:09:33.189 --> 01:09:35.680
So nice with Huffman
codes now you

01:09:35.680 --> 01:09:40.898
can compress this
one book to about--

01:09:40.898 --> 01:09:43.470
you can reduce it by 44%.

01:09:43.470 --> 01:09:46.380
So that's good.

01:09:46.380 --> 01:09:49.859
Then I thought maybe let's
compress another file.

01:09:49.859 --> 01:10:01.440
So I took this Apache Camel,
which is a Java library,

01:10:01.440 --> 01:10:03.540
and which looks
something like this.

01:10:03.540 --> 01:10:07.530
You have these function calls
and semicolons and symbols that

01:10:07.530 --> 01:10:09.780
don't occur as
commonly in English,

01:10:09.780 --> 01:10:12.210
but they occur more
commonly in code.

01:10:12.210 --> 01:10:22.830
And when we get
there, we'll see.

01:10:22.830 --> 01:10:26.280
So one thing you see here is
that see this parentheses,

01:10:26.280 --> 01:10:28.470
these parentheses get a
much shorter code word

01:10:28.470 --> 01:10:30.930
than they would have
gotten in normal English

01:10:30.930 --> 01:10:35.070
because a code has many more
parentheses than English.

01:10:35.070 --> 01:10:37.980
And similarly semicolon
is much shorter code word.

01:10:37.980 --> 01:10:40.795
Semicolon you see right
below the parentheses.

01:10:40.795 --> 01:10:42.420
So depending on your
data distribution,

01:10:42.420 --> 01:10:44.520
your Huffman code will
look very, very different.

01:10:44.520 --> 01:10:45.867
And that's the beauty of it.

01:10:45.867 --> 01:10:47.700
Any distribution you
create the optimal code

01:10:47.700 --> 01:10:49.650
for that distribution
and then use that one.

01:10:54.280 --> 01:10:56.890
Last one, here also I
tried to compress it.

01:10:56.890 --> 01:10:59.500
This one compressed
by about 40%.

01:10:59.500 --> 01:11:03.760
The last thing I did was
I took an encrypted file.

01:11:03.760 --> 01:11:06.280
So the encrypted file was not--

01:11:06.280 --> 01:11:07.450
it's bytes.

01:11:07.450 --> 01:11:09.160
It's not human readable English.

01:11:09.160 --> 01:11:11.680
So I made the Huffman
code on the bytes.

01:11:11.680 --> 01:11:14.170
Look at this Huffman code.

01:11:14.170 --> 01:11:17.080
Anybody tell me, what do you
see in this Huffman code?

01:11:22.560 --> 01:11:23.060
Yeah.

01:11:23.060 --> 01:11:26.690
You see that all of the
symbols have same depth.

01:11:26.690 --> 01:11:29.270
They seem to have
all the same depth.

01:11:29.270 --> 01:11:32.180
Can anybody explain why
the encrypted file gives me

01:11:32.180 --> 01:11:34.325
this strange looking
Huffman code?

01:11:38.360 --> 01:11:40.370
Why did all codewords
get the same length?

01:11:40.370 --> 01:11:42.320
This is basically
the fixed length code

01:11:42.320 --> 01:11:44.030
that we saw in
the first lecture.

01:11:46.910 --> 01:11:50.721
If it's not like this, then
you can basically [INAUDIBLE]..

01:11:53.290 --> 01:11:53.790
Yeah.

01:11:53.790 --> 01:11:56.150
So typically, most
encryption algorithms,

01:11:56.150 --> 01:12:00.860
you will see that their
output is IID uniform--

01:12:00.860 --> 01:12:03.680
it's uniformly spread
across the bytes.

01:12:03.680 --> 01:12:06.710
And the reason is something
like it should not

01:12:06.710 --> 01:12:09.080
leak any information about
the original distribution.

01:12:09.080 --> 01:12:12.680
So that's why all inputs go
to the uniform distribution

01:12:12.680 --> 01:12:15.500
for a good encryption algorithm.

01:12:15.500 --> 01:12:18.740
Here I think we used AES, which
is one of the state-of-the-art

01:12:18.740 --> 01:12:20.570
encryption algorithms.

01:12:20.570 --> 01:12:22.370
So you see all of these.

01:12:22.370 --> 01:12:25.235
The whole tree everything
is of the same length.

01:12:28.370 --> 01:12:32.630
So one lesson here is that you
can't compress encrypted data.

01:12:32.630 --> 01:12:36.200
You have this-- there's
just some fundamental

01:12:36.200 --> 01:12:37.135
incompatibility.

01:12:37.135 --> 01:12:39.260
After encryption if you
want to compress something,

01:12:39.260 --> 01:12:42.830
do it before encryption,
not after encryption

01:12:42.830 --> 01:12:44.630
because encryption
will basically destroy

01:12:44.630 --> 01:12:45.800
all structure in your data.

01:12:45.800 --> 01:12:48.290
The data will look completely
random to any compressor.

01:12:48.290 --> 01:12:50.760
It will not get any
compression at all.

01:12:50.760 --> 01:12:53.510
So if somebody comes to
you with a startup idea,

01:12:53.510 --> 01:12:57.080
I can compress every
file you give me by 50%,

01:12:57.080 --> 01:12:59.150
give them encrypted file.

01:12:59.150 --> 01:13:00.020
See what they do.

01:13:00.020 --> 01:13:02.510
And I guess if they're
able to compress

01:13:02.510 --> 01:13:05.780
an encrypted file, that
means they have broken AES.

01:13:05.780 --> 01:13:07.760
So that's a good thing.

01:13:07.760 --> 01:13:08.900
Most likely they won't.

01:13:11.780 --> 01:13:14.870
Is that clear that the
encrypted data is random?

01:13:14.870 --> 01:13:17.060
It's IID uniform.

01:13:17.060 --> 01:13:20.510
It's not exactly IID, but
it's close to IID uniform.

01:13:20.510 --> 01:13:24.680
Over all the bytes, 0 to 256, so
you will spend basically eight

01:13:24.680 --> 01:13:27.330
bits per symbol there
because each of the byte

01:13:27.330 --> 01:13:28.365
is equally likely.

01:13:33.640 --> 01:13:37.480
And then I actually ran
the compression right here.

01:13:37.480 --> 01:13:39.220
I think you can see.

01:13:39.220 --> 01:13:41.422
Let me try to zoom in.

01:13:41.422 --> 01:13:42.880
Basically, you get
compression rate

01:13:42.880 --> 01:13:45.910
of one, which means that
there is no compression at all

01:13:45.910 --> 01:13:47.530
for the encrypted file.

01:13:55.310 --> 01:13:56.390
So this is available.

01:13:56.390 --> 01:13:57.995
We'll post the slides.

01:14:02.920 --> 01:14:03.480
Let's see.

01:14:08.072 --> 01:14:10.280
So there are a couple more
things about Huffman codes

01:14:10.280 --> 01:14:13.040
that I will not
try to cover today.

01:14:13.040 --> 01:14:16.910
We will wrap them up
in the next lecture.

01:14:16.910 --> 01:14:21.050
But after we cover
those very small topics,

01:14:21.050 --> 01:14:24.620
the next lecture will mostly
be about theoretical intuition

01:14:24.620 --> 01:14:27.650
behind entropy and
block coding, why

01:14:27.650 --> 01:14:30.140
entropy, what is the
importance of entropy,

01:14:30.140 --> 01:14:32.930
why does block coding
works, how does

01:14:32.930 --> 01:14:36.380
it interact with laws of
large numbers, probability,

01:14:36.380 --> 01:14:37.650
and so on.

01:14:37.650 --> 01:14:41.780
So Professor Saki will
be taking that lecture.

01:14:41.780 --> 01:14:45.500
And next week, we will start
talking about practical block

01:14:45.500 --> 01:14:48.230
codes or stream
codes, which actually

01:14:48.230 --> 01:14:49.700
get very close to entropy.

01:14:49.700 --> 01:14:53.300
And they are the ones that are
used for the state-of-the-art

01:14:53.300 --> 01:14:56.270
basically compressors.

01:14:56.270 --> 01:14:59.810
So with that, thank you.

01:14:59.810 --> 01:15:02.650
Let me know if you
have any questions.

